2186045417] [17592186098574 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d5bdadae-1e47-5327-b074-0eb473615370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098575 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ff/ffbec42cd49ed7bb3c30b41e3a91b9c3aa592b0017813ea3debfc3e613b61b27.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098575 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098575 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ee17b9c8-c085-5838-a10c-520080108563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098576 #edb/ref 1080 "images/f1/f1a2f1b25cc02927d65d55de971b6579377629b570e08dad80d543a36e405cf9.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098576 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186098576 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fab4872e-1342-5416-9585-f66b6910a517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100668 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100668 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(3,4)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$. Find a point that must lie on $y=f(x)+3$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100668 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c6c748a-f067-527d-91aa-3ead12bb569d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100668 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nThe graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x)+3\n$$\n\nis obtained by translating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $3$ units *up*.\n\nHence, if the point $(3,4)$ lies on $y=f(x)$, then the point\n\n$$\n(3,4+3) = (3,7)\n$$\n\nlies on\n\n$$\ny=f(x) + 3\n$$\n\n![](images/57/577911187229d6a56a8885e4cea2e25752615ed7bb807768abf4b54c5073d177.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAs $(3,4)$ lies on $y = f(x)$, we have that\n\n$$\nf(3) = 4\n$$\n\nNow, if $x = 3$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= f(3) + 3 \\\\\n&= 4 + 3 \\\\\n&= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(3,7)$ must lie on\n\n$$\ny = f(x) + 3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100669 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100669 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below and the point $(0,9)$ lies on the curve. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)-2$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0c3acc57c74ced95e7387e285d9de1c629e7724fe9e111b13df209198069e8d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100669 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b856c88-45bf-54a5-80c4-134a7544742c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100669 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of $y = f(x)$ passes through the point $(0, 9)$ as shown in the diagram.\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)-2$ can be obtained from $y=f(x)$ by translating it by $2$ units *down*. So, it must pass through the point $(0,7)$.\n\nThe correct graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27de6227dee35cb8a7eaf2873409dfd6ad8c3c5f8d0cbd0de9ac223925b3fd76.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100670 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100670 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $A$ with coordinates $(2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$ and the point $B$ with coordinates $(2,-4)$ lies on the curve $y = g(x)$. If the curve $y = g(x)$ is related to $y = f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x) = f(x) + k$, then what is the value of $k$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100670 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85d2f012-deb3-55dc-8e38-51bfe62eab20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100670 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that the point $B(2, -4)$ lies $3$ units *below* the point $A(2, -1)$. So, the graph of $y = g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by shifting it $3$ units *down*.\n\n![](images/46/4656ae5ff75132fc9b760b6a8f73b0b434a8f66ecd621fa974a265df1d0d8a2c.png)\n\nAs a result, $k = -3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100712 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5007" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100712 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x) +2$ is shown below. Sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$?\n\n![](images/f5/f5404d5fbf52098eaa7161f0219e4df98a3ae9f5b6b7b6b0220089d62abff82d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100712 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1c57d35a-24ad-51ae-b00d-0f55396f045d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100712 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x) + 2\n$$\n\ncan be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by translating $2$ units *up*.\n\nSo, the graph $y = f(x)$ would be $2$ units *below* the graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(x) + 2\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0ce67d91fd6c15d97e882c785392f001a4f38ed9bedd50d8631b34e4d5543adc.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100847 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Points that Must Lie on a Translated Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100847 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100847 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ac31493b-30d0-5ed1-8422-f1f475e3c576" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100848 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100848 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100848 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Graph of a Translated Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100848 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100848 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a8224846-04e6-531d-9415-9a18d2afddb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100849 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100849 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100849 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Vertical Shift of a Translated Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100849 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100849 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "10557b22-4b4b-5860-abcb-0dcd96a5743d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100887 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186101384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100887 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Graph of an Original Function Given a Translated Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100887 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100887 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d982b565-2c41-5687-8d00-86b971bfb18b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1045 "Vertical Translations of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101091 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "fa3b0123-3cb4-59d6-8bc7-e2be683f501b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101206 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186098571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101206 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101206 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/82/82012efd1fbf78238ce1b6ecc49819283976db8f39ac5f57ec232a5eda8029dc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown above and the point $(0, 1)$ lies on the curve. Which of the following is the graph of $y = f(x) + 2$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101206 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c24cb6a5-e9b4-5b2a-b705-fcd1200bf566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101638 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101638 #edb/ref 1061 14720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101638 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f72e4e92-9df5-551e-bf9d-c6db356cd3c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101638 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101639 #edb/ref 1061 14721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101639 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f45cbb5-b569-58b3-95eb-fbb98b10614a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101639 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101640 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101640 #edb/ref 1061 14722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101640 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e7dee1eb-ff16-5f46-b872-2042f7c92dcc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101640 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101641 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101641 #edb/ref 1061 14723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101641 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba6cf136-6d59-5437-b6e3-d44b8ca10f7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101641 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101704 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101704 #edb/ref 1061 18128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101704 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0612808f-4124-5dbf-a60c-734f4636a504" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101704 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101705 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101705 #edb/ref 1061 18130 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101705 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f13d8d49-a65d-58fb-9ad0-f736406fedbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101705 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101945 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we have the graph of $y=f(x)$ shown below. What would the graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+2\n$$\n\nlook like, and how does it relate to the original curve?\n\n![](images/1a/1a55ebd7e9dc656f3a2488debf14e63e655ed7548b76f63c26498f09d96d65ca.png)\n\nFor each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+2\n$$\n\nwith the same $x$-value and a $y$-value that is two units greater. So let's create a table of points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=f(x)+2[/math] \\\\ (-2, 0) & (-2, 2) \\\\ (0, 1.5) & (0, 3.5) \\\\ (1, 2) & (1, 4) \\\\ (3,-3) & (3,-1)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery point on the graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+2\n$$\n\nis precisely two units above a point on the graph of $y=f(x)$. The graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+2\n$$\n\nis an **upward vertical translation** of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by two units.\n\n![](images/66/6636d65acf9cd34d818d3d9e12a7b7de36dec4ceb70c7503a08751f971868855.png)\n\nOn the other hand, for each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$ there is a corresponding point on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$ with the same $x$-value and a $y$-value that is two units smaller. So let's create a table of points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=f(x)-2[/math] \\\\ (-2, 0) & (-2,-2) \\\\ (0, 1.5) & (0,-0.5) \\\\ (1, 2) & (1, 0) \\\\ (3,-3) & (3,-5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEvery point on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$ is two units below a point on the graph of $y=f(x)$. The graph of $y=f(x)-2$ is a **downward vertical translation** of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by two units.\n\n![](images/72/72588aaa92f5b4bf9923c381cf5ad28ebb7288e77f6442549d0ffe1fdac3dc48.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101945 #edb/ref 1065 2809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101945 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "198f4c8f-65df-5b93-951c-12fd4d9dfd12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101945 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101972 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the solid curve $y=f(x)$ and the dashed curve\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+b\n$$\n\nshown below. What is the value of $b$?\n\n![](images/b0/b058da1ea31e45f3029f1542642d802a35da7280f315a37ea92044e5c322fbd4.png)\n\nThe graph of\n\n$$\ny=f(x)+b\n$$\n\ncan be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by translating it $6$ units down. So, $b=-6$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101972 #edb/ref 1065 3295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101972 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f2c0aba0-7e3b-5a75-83b0-7386793a015b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101972 #edb/ref 1067 "Identifying the Shift" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
